On 29/02/2016 10:59, Gleki Arxokuna wrote:
> I noticed that some people have trouble operating Skype.
> So here are the basic rules of a successful voice chat for Skype
>
> 1. Use a group where all potential speakers share their interest in=20
> the forthcoming conference in text form only. This group already=20
> exists. It's called {ko ciska po'o tavla ...} (meaning "Talk by=20
> writing only...") This group is only for planning, not for actual=20
> talking. Ask in this mail thread to add you there.
> 2. One person runs a desktop version of Skype and has good incoming=20
> and outcoming internet connection (~1Mbit/sec in both directions is fine)=
.
> 3. This person must add all interested people to his/her friend list=20
> and those people must confirm "friendship".
> 4. This person calls the first person and after successful connection=20
> drops other people into the chat window.
> 5. Please, everyone switch off your video by pressing on the video=20
> button so that it becomes crossed out.
> 6. Before any conference check your microphone and audio settings of=20
> Skype. Add "echo123" user (it's a bot) to your friend list, call it=20
> and follow the instruction. Change settings of your Skype client if=20
> necessary.
> 7. If you have noise around you during the conference or if other=20
> members notice a noise from your side switch off your microphone by=20
> pressing on the Microphone button so that it becomes crossed out.
>
> I think that as of now Skype is preferable since it has better voice=20
> compression and buffering mechanisms preventing clicks/pauses during=20
> conversation due to unstable internet connection. As of now it's=20
> unlikely that 100% of potential students of Lojban get normal internet=20
> connection. Many even change to mobile connection dropping the level=20
> of fast + stable internet connection even more.
>
> I should also note that QQ messenger used in China has been=20
> successfully tested by me several time with mainland China residents=20
> and it works okay. Just like Skype.
>
> Commercial clients win but explanations of that are outside the scope=20
> of this post.
